findings.finding:
My current finding is that a “shifty’’ α must satisfy a linear system: if we write a_i=α(i) for i=0,…,8 and let b_j=β(j) (only finitely many b_j are non‑zero), then for every integer n  

\[
\sum_{i=0}^{8}a_i\,b_{\,i-n}= 
\begin{cases}
1 & n=k\text{ or }n=l,\\[2mm]
0 & \text{otherwise},
\end{cases}
\tag{★}
\]

where k≠l are the two prescribed shifts.  Thus the existence of β is equivalent to the existence of an integer solution (b_j) of the finite linear system (★) whose unknowns are the b_j that appear for the finitely many n for which the sum can be non‑zero (i.e. n∈A−B).  Consequently, a is shifty iff the integer matrix with entries a_i (indexed by i and the relevant n) admits a solution to (★) with exactly two right‑hand‑side entries equal to 1.  This reduction to a finite integer linear system is the key step for counting all possible α.
